1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,082
Switch on this electromagnet,

2
00:00:02,122 --> 00:00:05,669
and the steel bar above it is pulled down
onto the poles.

3
00:00:05,709 --> 00:00:08,093
The bar carries no conduction current,

4
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:12,065
so the wire formula, F = I l × B, has
nothing to act on.

5
00:00:12,105 --> 00:00:15,434
Inside a motor it's the same. The copper
sits in slots,

6
00:00:15,474 --> 00:00:20,367
where the field is weak, and most of the
force lands on steel and magnets.

7
00:00:20,407 --> 00:00:23,124
So we need force from the field without
asking

8
00:00:23,164 --> 00:00:26,302
exactly where it pushes. The tool is
energy.

9
00:00:27,152 --> 00:00:29,596
First, the answers from Lesson 7.

10
00:00:29,636 --> 00:00:39,719
One: at 3000 rpm, ωe = 1257 rad/s, so the
phase EMF peaks at ωe λm = 8.17 V.

11
00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:44,145
Two: line to line, √3 times that: 14.1 V.

12
00:00:44,185 --> 00:00:49,425
Three: yes, a shorted motor brakes. No
energy can leave through the terminals,

13
00:00:49,465 --> 00:00:53,042
so the power the rotor gives up becomes
I²R heat.

14
00:00:53,082 --> 00:00:58,734
At 3000 rpm: about 110 A, 720 W, and 2.3
N·m of drag.

15
00:00:58,774 --> 00:01:03,677
Energy bookkeeping told us it must brake.

16
00:01:04,359 --> 00:01:06,957
So let's keep the books for the actuator:

17
00:01:06,997 --> 00:01:11,182
a 500-turn coil on a U-shaped steel core,
and a bar at gap x.

18
00:01:11,222 --> 00:01:15,319
Energy enters at the terminals. Some heats
the winding's resistance,

19
00:01:15,359 --> 00:01:20,061
some is stored in the magnetic field, and
some leaves as mechanical work.

20
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:24,435
Put the resistance outside, and what's
left is a lossless coupling field,

21
00:01:24,475 --> 00:01:27,491
with an electrical port and a mechanical
port.

22
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:33,713
The terminal voltage is v = R i + dλ/dt,
where λ is the flux linkage.

23
00:01:33,753 --> 00:01:37,801
Multiply by i dt: the field receives i dλ.

24
00:01:37,841 --> 00:01:42,783
Part of it is stored, dW_f, and the rest
is work on the bar, F dx.

25
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:48,581
Hold the bar still, at a 1 mm gap, and
raise the current. The flux linkage

26
00:01:48,621 --> 00:01:53,666
follows this curve: it bends because the
steel from Lesson 4 saturates.

27
00:01:53,706 --> 00:01:58,842
With no motion, all of i dλ is stored, so
the field energy is its integral:

28
00:01:58,882 --> 00:02:02,041
the area between the curve and the λ axis.

29
00:02:02,081 --> 00:02:08,158
The area under the curve is the integral
of λ di. It's called the co-energy, W′.

30
00:02:08,198 --> 00:02:12,799
Together they fill the rectangle λ i. At 6
A,

31
00:02:12,839 --> 00:02:18,050
that's 0.79 J of energy and 1.06 J of co-
energy.

32
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:22,455
On a straight line they'd be equal halves:
½ L i² each.

33
00:02:23,305 --> 00:02:26,997
Now let the bar close in by a small step.
At the smaller gap,

34
00:02:27,037 --> 00:02:30,540
the same current links more flux, so the
curve rises.

35
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:34,688
Hold λ constant during the step: no
electrical energy enters,

36
00:02:34,728 --> 00:02:36,932
so the work comes out of storage.

37
00:02:36,972 --> 00:02:41,584
The force is minus the slope of the field
energy, at constant λ.

38
00:02:41,624 --> 00:02:47,111
Hold i constant instead: the source adds i
dλ, and the work is exactly the gain

39
00:02:47,151 --> 00:02:52,659
in co-energy. The force is the slope of
the co-energy, at constant current.

40
00:02:52,699 --> 00:02:56,409
For a small step, the two slivers differ
only at second order,

41
00:02:56,449 --> 00:02:58,403
so both give the same force.

42
00:02:58,443 --> 00:03:03,378
Computed from these saturated curves at 1
mm and 6 A,

43
00:03:03,418 --> 00:03:07,672
both give 759.3 N, pulling the gap shut.

44
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:14,211
With linear iron, λ = L(x) i, and the co-
energy is ½ L i².

45
00:03:14,251 --> 00:03:20,330
So F = ½ i² dL/dx. The force follows the
slope of the inductance,

46
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:23,078
not the inductance itself.

47
00:03:23,118 --> 00:03:26,773
For our actuator, treated as a circuit:

48
00:03:26,813 --> 00:03:32,757
two gaps in series with 220 mm of steel. L
is 61 mH at 1 mm.

49
00:03:32,797 --> 00:03:36,425
L rises as the gap closes, so dL/dx is
negative:

50
00:03:36,465 --> 00:03:40,469
the force pulls the gap shut. At 1 A,
about 30 N.

51
00:03:40,509 --> 00:03:45,640
That assumes no leakage and no fringing.
Let's check it against a field solution.

52
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:51,458
Here's the actuator solved with Lesson 3's
finite-difference solver:

53
00:03:51,498 --> 00:03:53,820
2 mm gap, 1 A, linear steel.

54
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:56,458
Flux bulges out at the pole edges:
fringing.

55
00:03:56,498 --> 00:04:00,355
And some circles the coil without reaching
the bar: leakage.

56
00:04:00,395 --> 00:04:04,205
Leakage, plus some fringing, raises the
inductance

57
00:04:04,245 --> 00:04:08,355
well above the circuit model: 49 mH
instead of 31.

58
00:04:08,395 --> 00:04:13,569
But leakage hardly changes with the gap,
so it adds almost no force. The

59
00:04:13,609 --> 00:04:19,902
co-energy slope gives 8.23 N, 8 % above
the circuit model, mostly from fringing.

60
00:04:20,752 --> 00:04:25,005
Now an independent check. The field itself
carries stress:

61
00:04:25,045 --> 00:04:30,190
tension B²/2μ0 along the lines, and an
equal pressure across them.

62
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:36,030
Where lines leave steel at right angles,
they pull on its face.

63
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:40,223
At 1 mm and 1 A, 0.31 T pulls with 37 kPa.

64
00:04:40,263 --> 00:04:44,821
Integrate the full stress tensor on a
closed contour in the air around the bar,

65
00:04:44,861 --> 00:04:47,857
and you get the total force, fringing
included.

66
00:04:47,897 --> 00:04:50,164
From half a millimetre to 4 mm,

67
00:04:50,204 --> 00:04:56,137
it matches the co-energy slope within 0.2
%, whichever contour we choose.

68
00:04:56,987 --> 00:05:00,426
With saturating steel there's no single
inductance.

69
00:05:00,466 --> 00:05:04,682
Here are λ–i curves at three gaps, using
Lesson 4's steel.

70
00:05:04,722 --> 00:05:10,168
The force is still the change in co-
energy: at 6 A,

71
00:05:10,208 --> 00:05:17,890
the area between the 0.9 and 1.1 mm
curves, over 0.2 mm, gives 759 N.

72
00:05:17,930 --> 00:05:22,843
Against current, the force first grows as
i², then flattens.

73
00:05:22,883 --> 00:05:27,023
From 4 to 8 A, it grows only 1.86 times,
not 4.

74
00:05:27,063 --> 00:05:35,974
And ½ i² dL/dx fails both ways at 8 A:
about 1900 N with the unsaturated L,

75
00:05:36,014 --> 00:05:41,526
240 N with L = λ/i. The co-energy gives
895.

76
00:05:41,566 --> 00:05:46,712
This circuit model assumes uniform flux,
no leakage, and no hysteresis.

77
00:05:47,562 --> 00:05:52,883
Rotation works the same way: replace x
with an angle θ,

78
00:05:52,923 --> 00:05:58,447
and force with torque. T = ∂W′/∂θ at
constant current.

79
00:05:58,487 --> 00:06:00,881
A steel rotor between two poles gives an

80
00:06:00,921 --> 00:06:04,224
inductance that varies with angle, twice
per turn.

81
00:06:04,264 --> 00:06:10,306
With linear iron, T = ½ i² dL/dθ: the
rotor turns toward maximum inductance,

82
00:06:10,346 --> 00:06:15,167
where the gaps are smallest. That's
reluctance torque.

83
00:06:16,017 --> 00:06:18,721
A real motor has three windings and
magnets.

84
00:06:18,761 --> 00:06:22,887
With linear iron, its co-energy collects
three kinds of terms.

85
00:06:22,927 --> 00:06:26,488
Self and mutual inductances, times
products of currents.

86
00:06:26,528 --> 00:06:31,320
The magnet's flux linking each winding,
λm, times that winding's current.

87
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:35,113
And a term from the magnet alone, present
with no current at all.

88
00:06:35,153 --> 00:06:40,541
Differentiate with respect to θ, and each
kind gives its own torque. The first gives

89
00:06:40,581 --> 00:06:46,574
reluctance torque, the last gives cogging.
The middle one is the subject of Lesson 9.

90
00:06:47,424 --> 00:06:51,370
Try it yourself: the lesson's script
computes this force both ways.

91
00:06:51,410 --> 00:06:56,023
Then build one. Clamp the core poles down,
and rest the bar on a

92
00:06:56,063 --> 00:07:01,048
2 kg non-magnetic block on a kitchen
scale, 2 mm below the poles.

93
00:07:01,088 --> 00:07:04,373
Zero the scale, and drive a DC current.

94
00:07:04,413 --> 00:07:11,407
The model predicts the reading drops by
about 210 g at 0.5 A, and 840 g at 1 A.

95
00:07:11,447 --> 00:07:14,333
Against i², the drop should be a straight
line,

96
00:07:14,373 --> 00:07:17,372
a little steeper than the 2D model
predicts.

97
00:07:17,412 --> 00:07:24,723
Or use an LCR meter: about 60 mH at 1.5
mm, and 43 at 2.5. Half i² times

98
00:07:24,763 --> 00:07:32,511
that slope gives 8.7 N at 1 A, slightly
high because the step is large.

99
00:07:33,361 --> 00:07:35,641
Three predictions before next time.

100
00:07:35,681 --> 00:07:39,180
One: an inductance varies with position as
L0 + k x.

101
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:42,578
At a constant current I, what's the force?

102
00:07:43,278 --> 00:07:47,736
Two: with linear iron, double the current.
What happens to the force?

103
00:07:48,436 --> 00:07:50,342
Three: with a magnet in the machine,

104
00:07:50,382 --> 00:07:53,693
why is there a torque term that's linear
in current?

105
00:07:54,393 --> 00:07:59,273
Next time: torque in our permanent-magnet
motor, from exactly these terms.
